  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA slow moving frontal boundary extends from lower Michigan\neastward across southern Ontario, Lake America and across the\nAdirondacks and into central New England. This boundary will\ndrift across the region today, although far southern areas look\nto remain south of the front through the day. A wave of low\npressure will be moving along the front for today, which will\nhelp increase the coverage of showers and thunderstorms,\nespecially by this afternoon and into this evening.  \n\nBased off the latest 3km HRRR, highest coverage of showers and\nthunderstorms looks to be across the Adirondacks through the day\non Tuesday, This activity will shift towards the Mohawk Valley,\nCapital Region and Berkshires for late in the day and into the\nfirst part of tonight. While the strong dynamics aloft will\nallow for plenty of shear, there is question about how much\nsurface-based instability will be in place. Widespread clouds\nmay limit the instability and CAMs suggest that SBCAPE will be\nunder 500 J/kg. The highest amounts of CAPE will be late today\nacross far western areas, but the better instability may only be\nacross western and central NY. Although some strong storms may\nbe ongoing across these areas during the day today, they will\nlikely weaken as they head into our area. ML/AI Severe guidance\ncontinues to show the better potential for damaging wind gusts\nto the west of the area for today as well. A Marginal Risk\n(Level 1 of 5) for severe storms does extent into the western\nMohawk Valley, but this appears to be a low threat for today.\n\nWhile there will be some downpours within showers and\nthunderstorms today, the threat for flooding rainfall also\nappears limited at this time. Although total rainfall may exceed\nan inch across the Adirondacks (NBM probability of 1\" is 50-80%\nhere), it's much lower for the rest of the region. 2\"\nprobability is only as high as about 30% over the far northern\nparts of Herkimer and Hamilton Counties. 00z SPC HREF shows\nhourly rates should generally be under a half inch as well.\nWhile some ponding can't be ruled out on roadways, total\nrainfall and rainfall rates appear manageable at this time. \n\nThere should be a large gradient in temps today, which is due to\nthe frontal boundary that will be bisecting the area. Northern\nareas will see more clouds and precip and will be on the cooler\nside of the boundary and temps will be limited to the 50s to\nnear 60. Meanwhile, far southern areas should see more sun and\nwill top out in the lower 70s in valley areas, especially the\nmid Hudson Valley. Another day of mild weather is expected again\non Wednesday before some cooler weather starts building towards\nthe area for Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nStrong frontal boundary still looks on track to be crossing the\nregion during the end of the week. After widespread showers on\nFriday, the front looks to cross by Friday night from west to\neast. \n\nA strong upper level trough will be passing over the Northeast \nfor the weekend. The 00z GEFS continues very similar to the past\nfew runs, with 850 hpa temps dropping down to around -8 to -10 \nC, which is well below normal for mid October. It still shows\n500 hpa heights around 2 STD below normal, although this is\nfairly brief, as the trough will be rather progressive. As a\nresult, daytime temps will only be in the 30s across the high\nterrain on Saturday, with 40s for valley areas. After a cold\nSaturday night, highs on Sunday will moderate somewhat, but will\nstill be below normal. \n\nAlthough moisture will be limited behind the cold front, some \nlake enhanced and upslope precip may occur behind the front, \nallowing for some snow showers for western, northern and high \nterrain areas. While some snowflakes do look likely across the \nhigher terrain this weekend, total amounts look very minor at \nthis time. In addition, a strong pressure gradient will result \nin breezy to windy conditions, making it feel even colder \nthrough the weekend. Although the growing season has ended for \nthe entire region, a widespread freeze is likely Saturday night \nfor the entire area as well.",
